---
import Figure from "../Figure.astro";
---
<Figure width="full" class="sysfig">
  <div class="method clip" id="method">
    <video class="method-video" src="/video/sciel-method.mp4" poster="/video/sciel-method-poster.jpg"
      muted playsinline preload="metadata" data-end="25.9"
      aria-label="How SCiEL works. Run: one attempt in every deme, from the parent, its deme and the repertoire; a planner draws the choices from logged odds; a frozen LLM writes the child; novelty rejects repeats; an exact check scores it; eight demes run, the weaker half stops at 50% and 75% of the budget. Learn: controllers learn the odds from the journal, bandits from step one, a world model after about 60 steps, an RL step policy after about 200. Share: research cells write and run their own tools in Docker; a finding is tried in the receiving deme and enters the repertoire only if its e-value passes 1/alpha; the next run inherits the repertoire and re-tests each item."></video>
    <img class="clip-still" src="/video/sciel-method-end.jpg" alt="" hidden />
    <button class="copy clip-replay" type="button" hidden>Replay</button>
  </div>
  <Fragment slot="caption">
    <b>Figure 3.</b> How SCiEL works. For each attempt, SCiEL draws its choices with learned odds, the frozen model writes a
    program, and an exact checker scores it. SCiEL records every choice and its odds in a journal and learns the odds of
    the next choices from it. A finding enters the repertoire <i>R</i> only after a randomised trial in the deme that
    receives it. Because the next attempt depends on <i>R</i>, and <i>R</i> changes only after a test, the process is not
    a loop that repeats itself.
  </Fragment>
</Figure>
